PRODID:-//Monash Events//EN
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Centre for Health Economics Policy Symposium 2026
DTSTART:20260901T093000
DTEND:20260901T170000
DESCRIPTION:<em>Sustainable Care: The next decade of policy reform</em>\n\
 nThis symposium brings together researchers\, policymakers\, practitioners
  and consumers to examine how Australia’s health\, aged care and disabil
 ity systems need to evolve over the coming decade. The full-day conference
  will focus on the future of the care economy\; exploring:\n\nCombined\, t
 he sessions aim to identify practical\, evidence‑based reforms to streng
 then the long‑term sustainability and effectiveness of care systems\n\nR
 egistration\n\nRegistration is now open. Earlybird pricing will be availab
 le until 12 August.\n\n<a class="btn" href="https://events.humanitix.com/c
 entre-for-health-economics-policy-symposium-2026">Register</a>\n\nWhat doe
 s the future of the care economy hold\, and how are health\, aged care\, a
 nd disability policies evolving to meet the challenges of tomorrow?\n\nHow
  can we optimise the architecture of our care system? How can we balance u
 niversality\, means- testing\, and out-of-pocket costs to improve overall 
 wellbeing?\n\nHow do we define and measure productivity in a sector where 
 the primary output is wellbeing rather than just volume and profit?  How
  can better measurement support accountability\, smarter decisions and mor
 e person-centred care?\n\nPrices can provide strong incentives for both pa
 tients and providers. How should we set prices across the care economy? Ca
 n better price signals lift quality\, reduce unnecessary care and improve 
 the sustainability of the system?\n\nReflection on the day and priorities 
 for future research.\n\nHost\n\n<img alt="" height="175" src="https://www.
 monash.edu/__data/assets/image/0004/4373482/Tony-Scott.png" style="float: 
 right\; margin: 1em 0px 1em 1em\;" width="175"/>\n\nPProf Scott’s goal i
 s to influence health policy using high quality research.  He is a healt
 h economist and an elected Fellow of the Academy of the Social Sciences i
 n Australia\, Past President of the Australian Health Economics Society\, 
 and a former Board Director of the International Health Economics Associat
 ion\, ARC Future Fellow and NHMRC Principal Research Fellow.\n\nHis resear
 ch expertise include the evaluation of national health policy\, the behavi
 our of healthcare providers\, health and care labour markets\, funding and
  incentives\, with a focus on primary care. He has published in leading in
 ternational academic journals and impacted on policy through his academic 
 and policy research and work on government committees\n\n<br/>Keynote Spea
 ker\n\n<img alt="" height="175" src="https://www.monash.edu/__data/assets/
 image/0005/4373483/250130_BillShortenPortraits_HiRes-1-1.jpg" style="float
 : right\; margin: 1em 0px 1em 1em\;" width="175"/>\n\nProf Shorten became 
 Vice-Chancellor and President of the University of Canberra in January 202
 5\, after a career that included leadership roles with the Australian Work
 ers’ Union and 18 years serving federal politics\, including six as Lead
 er of the Opposition. Prof Shorten studied law at Monash University.<br/> 
 <br/>Prof Shorten’s belief in the transformative power of education drew
  him to UC and the role. He arrived at a time of significant change for th
 e university and the broader higher education sector. It is a circumstance
  he views as an opportunity to drive UC’s rejuvenation.\n\nSpeakers\n\n<
 img alt="" height="175" src="https://www.monash.edu/__data/assets/image/00
 09/4373496/Blair.png" style="float: right\; margin: 1em 0px 1em 1em\;" wid
 th="175"/>\n\nMr Comley commenced as the Secretary of the Department of He
 alth\, Disability and Ageing on 17 July 2023. He has served as the Secreta
 ry of the Commonwealth Department of Climate Change and Energy Efficiency 
 and the Secretary of the Commonwealth Department of Resources\, Energy and
  Tourism. Mr Comley has also served as Secretary of the NSW Department of 
 Premier and Cabinet\, NSW’s most senior public servant.\n\nPrior to thes
 e positions\, he held senior leadership positions in the Department of Tre
 asury and as the Acting Chief Executive of the Australian Office of Financ
 ial Management\, with responsibility for managing the Commonwealth’s deb
 t and derivative portfolio. Mr Comley has had extensive engagement with Co
 mmonwealth State relations\, including reform of the Federation and Health
  funding in his role as Secretary of the Department of Premier and Cabinet
  in the NSW Government.\n\n<img alt="" height="175" src="https://www.monas
 h.edu/__data/assets/image/0007/4373494/Stephen-Duckett.jpg" style="float: 
 right\; margin: 1em 0px 1em 1em\;" width="175"/>\n\nProf Duckett is a heal
 th economist with expertise in costing and pricing hospital services and t
 he design of hospital payment systems. He is an Honorary Enterprise Profes
 sor in the School of Population and Global Health and the Department of Ge
 neral Practice and Primary Care at the University of Melbourne. He is also
  a Fellow of the Academy of the Social Sciences in Australia\, the Austral
 ian Academy of Health and Medical Sciences and the Australian Institute of
  Company Directors.<br/> <br/>Professor Duckett is also currently Deputy C
 hancellor of RMIT University. He was made a Member of the Order of Austral
 ia in January 2023.<br/> <br/>He has held top health sector leadership pos
 itions in Australia and Canada\, and literally wrote the book on the Austr
 alian health care system (<em>The Australian Health Care System</em>\, Oxf
 ord University Press\, 6e\, 2022).\n\n<img alt="" height="175" src="https:
 //www.monash.edu/__data/assets/image/0007/4373485/Elizabeth-dreaming-2.jpg
 " style="float: right\; margin: 1em 0px 1em 1em\;" width="175"/>\n\nDr Dev
 eny leads the CHFA\, which is the national voice for health consumers.\n\n
 She has worked across digital health\, primary care\, and health funding\,
  always with a focus on equity\, access\, and making sure policy reflects 
 the voices of the people it affects — and while she’s not a health eco
 nomist\, she knows value for money when she sees it.\n\n<img alt="" height
 ="175" src="https://www.monash.edu/__data/assets/image/0006/4373493/Emily-
 Lanscar.png" style="float: right\; margin: 1em 0px 1em 1em\;" width="175"/
 >\n\nProf Lancsar commenced as the Chief Health Economist in the departmen
 t in April 2024. Prior to this\, Prof Lancsar spent 25 years in academia\,
  most recently as Head of the Department of Health Economics Wellbeing and
  Society at the Australian National University (ANU). She also served as A
 ssociate Dean (Policy and Practice) in the College of Health and Medicine 
 at the ANU from 2020–2022\, where she remains a Professor.<br/> <br/>Pro
 f Lancsar has also held academic appointments at Monash University (where 
 she remains an Adjunct Professor)\, the University of Newcastle Upon Tyne 
 in the United Kingdom\, the University of Sydney and University of Technol
 ogy\, Sydney. Prof Lancsar holds PhD\, Masters\, and Bachelors degrees in 
 Economics\, a Postgrad Diploma in Health Economics and Evaluation\, and a 
 Bachelor of Arts (Asian Studies).\n\n<!-- false -->\n<div class="accordion
  nested-accordion js-frontend-complex-accordion" id="Component-4373352" ro
 le="tablist">\n<div class="hidden">\n<span class="hidden nested-accordion_
